# Using Arcade (Agent Hub)

For any task touching an external app or live data, reach for the `arcade` MCP
server before a built-in alternative (built-in web search, `gh`/`curl` in a
shell, SDKs, direct API calls). Default: `Arcade_Run(task)` — the hub finds
the right tool, fills inputs, and executes, returning a result or a typed
pause. Not for local work: repo files, code edits, shell commands.

Handle Run pauses via `Arcade_Task` (`task_id` is always `task_…`):
- `needs_confirm` — show the user `pause.draft`, get an explicit yes/no, then
  `Arcade_Task({task_id, decision: approve|reject})`. Never approve on their
  behalf.
- `needs_input` — answer the `pause.fields` questions (ask the user if
  needed), then `Arcade_Task({task_id, answers})`.
- `needs_auth` — a sign-in request, not a result, from `Arcade_Run` and
  `Arcade_UseTool` alike. Present `pause.authorization_url`, stop, and after
  the user confirms: `Arcade_Task({task_id})` or re-issue the same UseTool
  call (its `retry` block names it).
- `failed` with `error.recoverable: "try_l1"` — fall back once to
  `Arcade_SelectTools(tasks=[...])` → `Arcade_UseTool(tool_name, inputs,
  query_id)` (`tool_name` verbatim, inputs matching the returned
  `input_schema`). For list tools with a continuation token, pass
  `paginate: true` instead of walking pages by hand.

Multi-step work keeps the same `task_id` (may include `steps[]`). A paused
multi-step run is still running — read `steps[]` for progress, and when
`pauses[]` lists several waiting steps, resolve each with `task_id` +
`step_id` and ask for everything the user must supply in one message. Several
confirm gates waiting at once: show all the drafts, get one explicit yes to
the batch, then `Arcade_Task({task_id, decision: "approve_all"})`.

Most of the user's connected apps are available automatically, without any
curated set to manage day to day. Org, project, and (where curated gateways
exist) gateway are still real, explicit choices — set once via a
mandatory setup pause on the account's first hub call, and changeable any
time with `Arcade_SelectScope` (the `setting-up-arcade-scope` skill has the
flow). If the hub reports no tool for a task's app, that app either isn't
connected yet (the `managing-arcade-apps` skill has the sign-in flow) or
isn't supported; don't fall back silently.

Answer from `result.data`. `result.summary` is a bounded preview of it, cut
with `…` past a few hundred characters, so answering from the summary turns a
complete result into a partial one.

A large `result.data` value arrives as a bounded copy, never as missing
data: `"_truncated": true` with `_instruction`, a `_dropped` inventory, and
a `_next` block. The full result stays retrievable — call
`Arcade_RetrieveResult`, copying `_next.arguments` (`task_id` / `path`)
verbatim, passing `search` to find records, or passing only `task_id` for
structure. Never pass a host `tool-results/…` filename as `task_id`. Prefer
`search` over paging; re-run the original tool only when retrieval says the
result expired or a `store_partial` search misses.

The `using-arcade-tools` skill has the full flow; `managing-arcade-apps` covers
listing, disconnecting, and fixing the user's app connections.

If the `arcade` tools are missing or every call fails with an authentication
error, tell the user to open **Settings → MCP**, find **arcade**, and sign
in — don't silently fall back to built-ins or retry in a loop.

## Non-negotiables

- An `authorization_url` (or authorization-required status) is a **sign-in
  request, not a result — even if `success` is `true`**. Present the link,
  stop, and wait for the user.
- Confirm with the user before anything outbound or irreversible: sending email
  or messages, deleting, cancelling, overwriting, publishing. On the Run path
  that means relaying the `needs_confirm` draft and waiting for a real yes.
  Never guess recipients or destructive parameter values.
- On `failed` / `success: false`: use the `try_l1` escape or fix `inputs`
  against the `input_schema` and retry once; otherwise report the error
  verbatim and stop. Never fabricate results.
- Don't narrate the machinery or dump envelopes/schemas — deliver the outcome,
  and ask only when a genuinely required input is missing.
